videos.script:
A phone's battery is the user's, not yours. The system knows it, and it acts like it. {{battery}}

Every time your app touches the network, the radio wakes up. And it stays awake for seconds after the last byte. {{radio}} An app that checks for news every minute keeps it awake all day. The user finds your name at the top of the battery screen, and uninstalls you.

So both systems decide when background work runs. Android puts a phone to sleep when nobody touches it, and ranks apps by how often they are used. {{android}} iOS runs background tasks at a time it chooses, and promises nothing about when. {{ios}}

In this lesson you read what each one allows, and the same daily sync written three ways. {{three}} Then you run a small model of a day: polling every minute against one batched job. {{model}} The numbers come from a stated assumption, and the gap between them is the lesson.
